<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">River Bann</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The River Bann is the longest river in Northern Ireland, flowing north through Lough Neagh to the Atlantic near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coleraine">Coleraine</a>. In early medieval Ireland it marked the western limit of the Ulaid after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ui-neill">Uí Néill</a> pushed them east, so that the Kingdom of Ulaid was confined to Antrim and Down.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/river-bann.jpg" alt="River Bann" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hellish at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:River_Bann_at_Coleraine.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
